  "_id": "6a7f5797757ee51246381c53",
  "lab": "6a7f5796eda1a5feaff0b458",
  "name": "C-PEPTIDE",
  "code": "C PEP",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>C-peptide Level (ng/mL or pmol/L)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Levels</div></td><td><div>Normal C-peptide levels indicate that the pancreas is functioning properly and producing an appropriate amount of insulin. Normal C-peptide levels help confirm the endogenous production of insulin and can be useful in differentiating between type 1 and type 2 diabetes.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>Elevated C-peptide levels can be observed in conditions where there is an excess production of insulin, such as insulinoma (a tumor of the pancreas) or in individuals with insulin resistance. High C-peptide levels may also be seen in certain medications that stimulate insulin release. Further evaluation and testing are necessary to identify the underlying cause of elevated C-peptide levels.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Low Levels</div></td><td><div>Low C-peptide levels may indicate a reduced production of insulin by the pancreas. This can be seen in type 1 diabetes, where there is an autoimmune destruction of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas. Low C-peptide levels can also be observed in some cases of type 2 diabetes, especially in the later stages of the disease when the pancreas may become less able to produce insulin.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
